### 2.1 Latency

Latency differs significantly between healthy and degraded endpoints.

GET /api/v1/items/\{id\} has a P99 latency of only **95 ms**, while GET /api/v1/search has a P99 latency of **280 ms** and GET /api/v1/health has a P99 latency of just **12 ms**.

In contrast, POST /api/v1/checkout reaches a P99 latency of **6800 ms**, showing severe tail\-latency degradation.

POST /api/v1/reports/export also shows very high latency, including an average error latency of **10200 ms**.

This indicates that the performance problem is concentrated in endpoints that heavily depend on saturated database connections.

### 2.2 Traffic

Traffic alone is not causing the degradation.

GET /api/v1/items/\{id\} handles **580 RPS**, which is higher than the **340 RPS** handled by POST /api/v1/checkout, but the items endpoint remains healthy.

Similarly:

- GET /api/v1/search: **420 RPS**
- POST /api/v1/checkout: **340 RPS**
- GET /api/v1/health: **60 RPS**
- POST /api/v1/reports/export: **45 RPS**

The degraded reports endpoint processes only **45 RPS**, demonstrating that high traffic alone is not responsible for the failures.

